Provenance
----------

bolt8 — clean-room implementation from BOLT#8 ("Encrypted and
Authenticated Transport", `lightning/bolts`), covering: "Cryptographic
Messaging Overview", "Authenticated Key Agreement Handshake", "Handshake
Versioning", "Noise Protocol Instantiation", "Authenticated Key Exchange
Handshake Specification" (Handshake State / Handshake State Initialization
/ Handshake Exchange — Acts One/Two/Three), "Lightning Message
Specification" (Encrypting and Sending / Receiving and Decrypting
Messages), and "Lightning Message Key Rotation". The CODE is this module's
own: no reference implementation's source (lnd's `brontide`,
core-lightning, eclair, ldk, etc.) was ported or consulted as a design
reference — it was built directly from the BOLT#8 specification text.

What is NOT this module's own is the specification text it quotes and the
Appendix A vectors it vendors, and that is what the entry below is for. The
sentence this paragraph replaced called BOLT#8 "not a copyrightable work
(merger doctrine; see CONVENTIONS.md §5)" — an argument this repository does
not make about the identical upstream anywhere else.

This module is built ON this repo's own `noise` module (`../noise`,
`Suite(DH, Cipher, Hash).SymmetricState`) — a from-scratch, clean-room
implementation of the (also public, also non-copyrightable) Noise Protocol
Framework specification. `noise` has no module-local NOTICE of its own;
its provenance is recorded in `../noise/README.md`'s `Provenance:` statement.
Nothing in `noise` is Lightning-specific and nothing in this module was
ported from `noise` (it is imported as a sibling dependency, per this
repo's `deps` convention).

The official BOLT#8 Appendix A "Transport Test Vectors" (`kat_vectors.
zig`) were fetched directly from `lightning/bolts`, raw GitHub, `master`
branch, for this pass:

  - `08-transport.md`
    (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/lightning/bolts/master/08-transport.md)

This is a public specification/test artifact — the BOLT's own published
conformance vectors — not copied from any Lightning implementation's test
suite. Every hex constant transcribed into `kat_vectors.zig` was
independently verified (exact byte length + exact substring match against
the fetched source file) before being embedded, not merely eyeballed. The
pre-Act-One chaining key (`ck_after_init`) and transcript hash were
additionally recomputed from scratch via Python `hashlib` directly from
BOLT#8's own "Handshake State Initialization" formulas (SHA-256 only, no
Lightning-specific or Noise-specific library code) as an independent
cross-check, not merely a second run of this module's own Zig code — see
`SPEC.md`'s "Verification" section.

`std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1` (field/scalar arithmetic, point group law,
`basePoint`, `mul`, `fromSec1`/`toCompressedSec1`), `std.crypto.aead.
chacha_poly.ChaCha20Poly1305` (via `noise.CipherState`), and `std.crypto.
hash.sha2.Sha256` are used via their public APIs (MIT, part of Zig
itself). The `dh.zig` adapter's `fromSec1 -> mul -> toCompressedSec1 ->
Sha256.hash` chain mirrors this repo's own `sphinx` module's IDENTICAL
BOLT#4 `ECDH` primitive and `bip340`'s secp256k1 key-pair conventions —
both already verified byte-exact against `std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1`
elsewhere in this repo; no new curve-arithmetic pattern was introduced.

**Copyleft:** NONE-SHIPPED — no GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied
anywhere in this module, and none is shipped with it. The Lightning reference
implementations named above were not read at all; the only third-party material
here is `lightning/bolts`' own specification text and test vectors, CC-BY 4.0,
which is permissive and attached below.

⭐ That sentence existed before 2026-09-09 in plain prose. It became a GATED
line the moment this file's kind changed from `provenance note` to
`third-party attribution`: `check-copyleft` only holds attribution files to the
formal shape, so declaring the condition honestly is what pulled the file under
a check it had never been under. Worth knowing before anyone changes a kind
back to make a gate quiet.

**Status: complete.** The act framing (`act.zig`), the secp256k1 DH
adapter (`dh.zig`), the full three-act `Noise_XK` handshake driver
(`handshake.zig`, including the six act-driver crypto cores
`Initiator.genAct1`/`.readAct2`/`.genAct3` and
`Responder.readAct1`/`.genAct2`/`.readAct3`, filled in from the BOLT#8
spec text alone in the 2026-07-12 fill-in pass), and the entire
post-handshake transport including key rotation (`transport.zig`) are
implemented and KAT-tested byte-exact against the official BOLT#8
Appendix A vectors, including all published negative vectors — zero
skipped tests. See `SPEC.md`'s "TODO(fable) — done record" for the
per-function verification detail.

